From: "Peter C. McCluskey" <pcm@rahul.net>
Date: Fri, 2 Dec 1994 07:49:20 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-72] ECON: Inflation

 dkrieger@netcom.com  (Dave Krieger) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-12-36:
>At 10:38 AM 12/1/94, Plaz wrote:
>>At 11:19 AM 12/1/94 -0500, Phil Goetz wrote:
>>>It's my impression that the vast majority of economists would agree with
>>>the first poster:  printing money causes inflation.
>>
>>Printing money without creating *value* causes inflation.
>  How do you create
>value just by printing money?  Money itself is useful as a medium of
>exchange (and hence loans bear interest), but increasing the money supply
>in excess of the overall growth of the economy _does_ cause inflation.

 That's almost right (except for that absurd claim about the usefullness of
money causing interest to exist). It is much simpler to think of the value
of money being determined by supply and demand, like any other commodity.
The growth of the economy is sometimes a good proxy for the demand for money,
but that demand can change independantly of the economy for reasons such as
changes in the expected inflation rate, or technological changes which affect
the way money is used. Digicash may have a big effect on the way that money
is used, so that trying to follow a simplistic rule like "issue money in
proportion to GDP" may be dangerous.
 The kind of rule that we need is not one that attempts to plan what the
money supply will be, but one which motivates the issuing bank(s) to keep
the value of the money stable.
