From: Godshatter <mentat@telerama.lm.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Dec 1994 11:15:48 +0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-12-73] singularity

On Thu, 1 Dec 1994, Nick Szabo wrote:

> I can't believe this kind of patent nonsense, long ago refuted on
> this list, has made it into a "FAQ":
> 
> Pete McCluskey:
> > Human history has been characterized by an accelerating rate of
> > technological progress.  
> 
> It is also characterized by decelerating rates of technologoical
> progress.  Technology follows s-curves, not exponentials or hyperbolics.
> 
> > Q. When will the Singularity occur?
> > 
> > The short answer is that the near edge of the Singularity is due about
> > the year 2035 AD.  One indication is human population growth, which
> > over the past 10,000 years has been following a hyperbolic trend.
> 
> This is also false to fact.  Since at least 1970, human population growth
> has fallen far off this trend, with growth rate decreasing substantially.
> This total pattern lags the pattern for particular countries going from 
> underdeveloped to to developed, which is again an S-curve.
> 

     Your point about population growth is correct, as is the fact that 
technologies follow S curves rather than progressing eternally.  But that 
fact only applies to specific technologies: space ships, radios, 
typewriters, etc.  This has nothing to do with the singularity.  To think 
that because specific technologies run up against intrinsic limits and to 
suggest that this invalidates the singularity - however defined - is to 
suggest that data could not be sent from one place to another above a 
certain speed because of the intrinsic speed limit of Moorse code, or 
that to get the computing capacity of a human brain, you would need a 
billion vacuum tubes.

     It is not the specific technology that counts.  Since the computing 
power of the human brain fits neatly into a human skull, and since it is 
pretty obvious that their some real speedups that are easy to think of and 
since these brains can be connected to one another via ever more advanced 
methods, and since - if we know how to speed up these slow neural 
processes, we will certainly be able to add more of that same capacity, 
then you will get an increase of intelligence, which is the basis of all 
your projections.  So you are no longer plotting a curve, but moving the 
whole curve upward.  How long this process will take is debatable, but 
even without direct neural modification, things are changing rapidly and 
are accelerating with no turning of the S curve in sight.  

