>this need not be the case. I don't really see how the same can be true
>of a lot of coal strip mines, which seem to be virtually not affected
>at all by the growing environemntal movement.)

I recall, around 1983, reading a US government book of statistics that
estimated the number of premature deaths in the US due to pollution from
coal-burning at 30K/yr.  This was the same number that was being floated
around as the number of premature deaths that would eventually be caused
by the Chernobyl disaster.  So I told people that if we replaced all
burning of coal by nuclear power, we could have one Chernobyl incident
per year and still break even.  Everyone I told this to thought I was joking,
even given the figures, presumably because nuclear power frightened them
in a way that "safe" coal didn't.


There was an article in Science News, Oct 1 1994, that is also relevant:

"Radioactivity From Burning Coal" <excerpt>

In 1982, US coal-burning power plants, which collectively consumed
616 million tons of coal, released 801 tons of uranium and 1971 tons
of thorium into the environment.  Roughly 11,400 pounds of the uranium
was U-235.  Moreover, global combustion of 2800 million tons of coal
that year released 8960 tons of thorium and 3640 tons of uranium,
of which 51,700 pounds was U-235.

Ironically, in 1982, 111 US nuclear power plants used 540 tons of
nuclear fuel to generate electricity.  Thus, "the release of nuclear
components from coal combustion far exceeds the entire US consumption
of nuclear fuels."


