From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Fri, 2 Dec 1994 17:57:26 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-12-80] BASICS: saving the wilderness

OK, I think I'm beginning to see how this works, and it fits into my
interest in *transitions* from Big Government to littler or no government.
Tell me if I have this (Extropianly- )straight:

You don't need to have a government to preserve, say, Yellowstone National
Park from being razed for oil. You create a Yellowstone Park foundation and
give or sell it to a private company who has an interest in preserving the
environment. That company gets the park and all of the currently
government-owned tourist facilities. They have a financial interest in
preserving the wilderness by limiting access and limiting their development
because they will lose the business otherwise. Presumably, Yellowstone is
worth more as a long-term investment as a tourist attraction than it would
be as a source of oil.

With a scenario like this, we lose a few corners of national parks, the
ones that are on very rich resources. But, people might pay more to live on
conservation land. But, more scenic mountaintops will get cable cars. But,
if owners charge a fee for access, some sites will become less crowded. 

Our national parks are such zoos now, it might be an improvement...


