At 11:39 PM 12/1/94, Jordan Sparks wrote:
>Just imagine how many
>people you would have to contact for permission to drive across town if
>there were no public land.

Think of a privatized city as a scaled-up mall. A mall where there was no
access from one store to another without going outside would not be able to
collect high rents. Similarly, real estate developments without access or
public space will not be able to sell or rent as many units as those that
solve these problems. Both residences and businesses are worthless without
reasonable access. The absence of coercion need not imply restricted travel
or the absense of "public" space. It simply requires a non-coercive
solution to these needs.

